body:
Sorry for not being clear.

By "in queue", I mean, the message has been sent by the main thread, but has not already been receieved by onmessage _in the current caculation round_, where I might have used the ambiguous term "passively".
For example, in the worker thread:

```
onmessage = evt =>
{
    //I want to check if there're multiple unhandled messages, if yes, then obsolete all non-latest messages
    if (areThereAnyLaterMessagesInQueue())
        return;

    //Doing some very expansive caculations.
    //...
}
```

If you don't want to stop evaluating what's currently on the stack you can very well make this yourself by wrapping your code in a `setTimeout`  and cancel any running timeout in the message handler.   
This way the next queued message will cancel the previous queued timeout and you'll get only the latest message to be executed.  
Something like
```js
let timeout = null;
onmessage = (evt) => {
  clearTimeout(timeout); // invalidate waiting messages
  timeout = setTimeout(() => {
    doSomethingWith(evt.data);
  }, 1); // 1ms should be enough to deal with a good load of messages
};
```
([Live demo](https://worker-pick-last-task.glitch.me))

Though this exact setup probably means that your Worker would handle a single task, which is not recommended, so you may prefer to handle it from a MessageChannel so that you can handle other tasks "normally" from the same Worker.
